## 4. What the GIS agent needs (inputs) and produces (outputs)

**Needs**
- Copernicus GLO-30 tiles for the chosen area (public AWS bucket; internet on in Kaggle)
- Sentinel-2 imagery for two comparable periods (Planetary Computer or Earth Engine), for the land-cover layer
- District boundaries (for example GADM level 2) and OpenStreetMap roads/villages, for exposure and place names
- The zone geometry rules and `zones_reference.csv` (already in hand)

**Produces**

| Output | Contents | Used by |
|---|---|---|
| `terrain_zones.csv` | `zone_id`, `mean_slope`, `elevation_range` (plus max slope and elevation stats) for every zone we serve | Risk Agent (features) |
| `zones.geojson` | Zone polygons (WGS84 lat/lon) with terrain, `veg_loss_pct`, `vuln_index`, `vuln_class`, `district`, place name | Map, Alert Agent |
| Raster overlays | Slope and vegetation-change images as PNGs, with a `bounds.json` giving their coordinates | Map |
| `exposure` fields or file | Per zone: villages/settlements and road length inside it | Alert Agent, map |
| `provenance.json` | Data sources, dates, resolution, what is derived and what is simulated | Everyone (PRD requires this) |
| `gis_agent.py` | The functions that recompute or fetch all of the above | Backend |
| README | How to use it (Section 8) | Teammate |

